// A local `let` whose annotation contradicts its value.
//
// The self-hosted compiler ACCEPTED this, in every shape, until
// 2026-08-20 -- and compiled and ran it. The parser read the annotation
// only to find where it ended, then discarded it, so no later stage
// could have checked it. The real compiler rejected all of these the
// whole time.
//
// Nothing found it because nothing in this repo annotates a local: not
// the parser corpus, not the execution corpus, not the examples, and
// not the compiler's own 30,000 lines. It was reachable only by
// writing Plum the way a user would. Hence this fixture.

let main (): Unit = {
    let a: Int = true;
    println(a.to_string())
}
